@class Class

Class is the base of `Evented`, `Handler`, `Icon` and `Control`, and through them of almost all of the Leaflet classes documented here.

Leaflet classes are plain JavaScript classes: extend them with `extends` and put setup code in `constructor()`, after calling `super()`. Class adds what plugins need on top of that: default options, mixins and constructor hooks.


@example

```js
class MyClass extends Class {
    constructor(greeter) {
        super();
        this.greeter = greeter;
    }

    greet(name) {
        alert(this.greeter + ', ' + name)
    }
}

// create instance of MyClass, passing "Hello" to the constructor
const a = new MyClass("Hello");

// call greet method, alerting "Hello, World"
a.greet("World");
```

Leaflet 1.x classes used an `initialize()` method instead of `constructor()`. Constructing a class that still has an `initialize()` method throws an error.

@section Options
@example

Declare the default options of a class with a static `defaultOptions` field. They are merged with the default options of the parent class instead of replacing them, which makes managing configuration of objects and default values convenient:

```js
class MyClass extends Class {
    static defaultOptions = {
        myOption1: 'foo',
        myOption2: 'bar'
    };
}

class MyChildClass extends MyClass {
    static defaultOptions = {
        myOption1: 'baz',
        myOption3: 5
    };
}

const a = new MyChildClass();
a.options.myOption1; // 'baz'
a.options.myOption2; // 'bar'
a.options.myOption3; // 5
```

Every instance gets its own `options` object, which inherits from the default options of its class. Changing a default later, like `Marker.defaultOptions.riseOnHover = true`, affects all instances that don't set the option themselves. `mergeOptions()` adds or changes defaults of an existing class without touching the defaults of its parent, which makes it the way to change defaults from a plugin:

```js
MyChildClass.mergeOptions({myOption4: true});
```

There's also [`Util.setOptions`](#util-setoptions), a method for
conveniently merging options passed to constructor with the defaults
defined in the class:

```js
class MyClass extends Class {
    static defaultOptions = {
        foo: 'bar',
        bla: 5
    };

    constructor(options) {
        super();
        Util.setOptions(this, options);
        ...
    }
}

const a = new MyClass({bla: 10});
a.options; // {foo: 'bar', bla: 10}
```

Note that the options object allows any keys, not just
the options defined by the class and its base classes.
This means you can use the options object to store
application specific information, as long as you avoid
keys that are already used by the class in question.

@section Mixins
@example

You can add methods and properties from other objects using the `include` method (such objects are called mixins). An `options` property of the mixin is merged into the default options of the class:

```js
const MyMixin = {
    foo() { ... },
    bar: 5
};

class MyClass extends Class {}

MyClass.include(MyMixin);

const a = new MyClass();
a.foo();
```


@section Constructor hooks
@example

If you're a plugin developer, you often need to add additional initialization code to existing classes (e.g. editing hooks for `Polyline`). Leaflet comes with a way to do it easily using the `addInitHook` method:

```js
MyClass.addInitHook(function () {
    // ... do something in constructor additionally
    // e.g. add event listeners, set custom properties etc.
});
```

You can also use the following shortcut when you just need to make one additional method call:

```js
MyClass.addInitHook('methodName', arg1, arg2, …);
```

Hooks added to a class also run for instances of its subclasses, parent class hooks first, and each hook runs once per instance. For Leaflet's own classes, they run after the constructor has finished, so a hook added to `Layer` sees a fully built `Polygon`. For a class of your own that extends a Leaflet class, they run before the constructor body of your class.

If other code adds hooks to a class of yours and these hooks need the constructor of your class to have run, mark the class with `static initHooks = true` and end its constructor with `this.callInitHooks()`:

```js
class MyLayer extends Layer {
    static initHooks = true;

    constructor(options) {
        super();
        Util.setOptions(this, options);
        this._items = [];
        this.callInitHooks();
    }
}
```
